  "nameStory": "This wetland wildflower gets its name from two sources: 'Virginia' points to where it was first scientifically described, and 'marsh' tells you exactly where to look for it. The 'St. John's-wort' part comes from the traditional blooming time around St. John's Day (June 24th), though this species actually flowers a bit later in summer than its upland cousins.",
  "description": "Virginia marsh St. John's-wort is a delicate wetland wildflower that loves to get its feet wet. This slender plant typically grows 1-2 feet tall with small, cheerful yellow flowers that brighten up soggy meadows and pond edges. Unlike its more robust upland relatives, this marsh-lover has a gentle, almost fragile appearance with narrow leaves and dainty blooms. It's a native New England plant that adds sunny splashes of color to our region's wetlands from mid-summer into early fall.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Flowers",
      "description": "Bright yellow flowers with 5 petals and numerous thread-like stamens that create a fuzzy center. Each flower is about half an inch across and has a delicate, papery texture when you gently touch the petals.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaves",
      "description": "Narrow, oval leaves arranged in opposite pairs along the stem. The leaves feel thin and smooth, and when held up to light, you can often see tiny translucent dots scattered throughout.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Habit",
      "description": "Grows as an upright, branching plant that's noticeably more slender and delicate-looking than other St. John's-worts. The whole plant has an airy, graceful appearance.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Fruit/Seeds",
      "description": "Small, reddish-brown capsules that split open to release tiny seeds. The capsules often persist into fall, giving the plant a rusty appearance after flowering.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Virginia marsh St. John's-wort in wet meadows, along pond and stream edges, in boggy areas, and other consistently moist soils throughout New England. It thrives in full sun to partial shade where the ground stays soggy or at least consistently damp. You'll often find it growing alongside other wetland plants like sedges, rushes, and swamp milkweed.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "New growth emerges as the weather warms, showing fresh green shoots in wet areas. Plants are building up energy for summer flowering.",
    "summer": "Peak blooming time from July through August, when the cheerful yellow flowers are most abundant and easiest to spot. This is the best time for identification.",
    "fall": "Flowers fade and seed capsules develop, often turning reddish-brown. The plant begins to die back but capsules may persist, helping with late-season identification.",
    "winter": "Dies back to the ground, leaving little trace above ground. The root system survives underground to sprout again in spring."
  },
  "funFact": "Virginia marsh St. John's-wort is like a natural mood ring for wetlands - it only thrives where the soil stays consistently moist, making it a reliable indicator that you've found a healthy wetland ecosystem. If you see this plant blooming happily, you know the water table is high and the habitat is in good shape!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other small St. John's-worts, but remember the 'marsh' part of its name - if you're not in a wet area, it's probably a different species. The wetland habitat is your biggest clue, since most other St. John's-worts prefer drier ground.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite its name referencing Virginia, this plant is actually native throughout much of eastern North America, including all of New England. The Virginia connection comes from early botanical naming conventions, not from the plant being limited to that state.",
  "compareHints": "Virginia marsh St. John's-wort stands out from its relatives by its love of wet feet - most other St. John's-worts in New England prefer dry to moderately moist soils. It's also more delicate-looking than the robust Common St. John's-wort and lacks the tiny, scale-like leaves of pineweed. When in doubt, check the habitat: if it's soggy, you've likely found the marsh species.",
